{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The moose (North America) or elk (Eurasia), "Alces alces", is the largest extant species in the deer family.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Moose are distinguished by the broad, flat (or "palmate") antlers of the males; other members of the family have antlers with a dendritic ("twig-like") configuration.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Moose typically inhabit boreal forests and temperate broadleaf and mixed forests of the Northern Hemisphere in temperate to subarctic climates. Hunting and other human activities have caused a reduction in the size of the moose's range over time.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Moose have been reintroduced to some of their former habitats.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Currently, most moose are found in Canada, Alaska, New England, Fennoscandia, Baltic States, and Russia.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Their diet consists of both terrestrial and aquatic vegetation.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The most common moose predators are the gray wolf along with bears and humans. Unlike most other deer species, moose are solitary animals and do not form herds.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Although generally slow-moving and sedentary, moose can become aggressive and move quickly if angered or startled.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Their mating season in the autumn features energetic fights between males competing for a female.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Dafeng () is a coastal district under the administration of Yancheng, Jiangsu province, China.","title":"Dafeng District"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Located on the Jiangsu North Plain with a coastline of 112 km , Dafeng was historically one of the largest salt-making areas in China and now is famed for its well preserved eco-system and numerous national conservation parks.","title":"Dafeng District"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The district has the largest national nature reserve for a rare deer species, Père David's Deer or Milu (麋鹿 ) in Chinese.","title":"Dafeng District"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It borders the prefecture-level city of Taizhou to the southwest.","title":"Dafeng District"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Europe, the Caucasus Mountains, Asia Minor, Iran, western and central Asia, the Atlas Mountains of north‑western Africa, and introduced populations in Australia, New Zealand, the United States, Canada, Peru, Uruguay, Chile and Argentina.
Evidence: {"sentence_id":1,"text":" The red deer inhabits most of Europe, the Caucasus Mountains region, Asia Minor, Iran, parts of western Asia, and central Asia.","title":"Red deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":2,"text":" It also inhabits the Atlas Mountains region between Morocco and Tunisia in northwestern Africa, being the only species of deer to inhabit Africa.","title":"Red deer"}
Evidence: {"sentence_id":3,"text":" Red deer have been introduced to other areas, including Australia, New Zealand, United States, Canada, Peru, Uruguay, Chile and Argentina.","title":"Red deer"}"],"previous_answer":"noanswer","question":"What are some other areas that share a common deer species with the forests of Mara and Mondrem?"}

{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Serena Williams tennis career began at 1995 but didn't compete in 1996.","title":"Serena Williams's early career"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She continued through the 1997 season and ended in the top 20 in 1998.","title":"Serena Williams's early career"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Williams sisters are two professional American tennis players: Venus Williams (b. 1980), a seven-time Grand Slam title winner (singles), and Serena Williams (b. 1981), twenty-three-time Grand Slam title winner (singles), both of whom were coached from an early age by their parents Richard Williams and Oracene Price.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" There is a noted professional rivalry between them – between the 2001 US Open and the 2017 Australian Open tournaments, they met in nine Grand Slam singles finals.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They became the first two players, female or male, to play in 4 consecutive grand slam singles finals from the 2002 French Open to the 2003 Australian Open; Serena famously won all 4 to complete the first of two "Serena Slams".","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Between 2000 and 2016, a 17-year span, they collectively won 12 Wimbledon singles titles (Venus won 5 and Serena won 7).","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" By winning the 2001 Australian Open women's doubles title, they became the 5th pair to complete the Career Doubles Grand Slam and the only pair to complete the Career Doubles Golden Slam.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" At the time, Venus and Serena were only 20 and 19 years old, respectively.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Since then they have gone on to add another two Olympic gold medals in the 2008 Beijing Olympics and the 2012 London Olympics.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Nearly a decade later, the duo would go on to win 4 consecutive grand slam doubles titles from 2009 Wimbledon through 2010 Roland Garros, which would catapult them to co-No.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" 1 doubles players on 7 June 2010.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Two weeks later, on 21 June 2010, Serena would hold the No. 1 singles ranking and Venus would be right behind her at No. 2 in singles.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Their most recent grand slam doubles titles came at the 2012 Wimbledon & 2016 Wimbledon events.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" They remain very close, often watching each other's matches in support, even after one of them has been knocked out of a tournament.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mariaan de Swardt (born 18 March 1971) is a former tennis player from South Africa, who played as a professional from 1988 to 2001.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She twice represented her native country at the Summer Olympics, in 1992 and 1996, and was a member of the South African Fed Cup Team in 1992 and 1994–1997.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 2006, de Swardt became a U.S. citizen.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles was the women's doubles event of the hundred-and-thirteenth edition of Wimbledon, the third Grand Slam of the year, the most prestigious tournament on grass courts, and the oldest tennis tournament in the world.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Martina Hingis and Jana Novotná were the defending champions but only Novotná competed that year with Natasha Zvereva.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Novotná and Zvereva lost in the semifinals to Mariaan de Swardt and Elena Tatarkova.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mariaan de Swardt won in the final 3–6, 7–6, 7–5 against Barbara Schett.","title":"1998 Boston Cup – Singles"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lisa Raymond and Rennae Stubbs won in the final 6–4, 6–4 against Mariaan de Swardt and Mary Joe Fernández.","title":"1998 Boston Cup – Doubles"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Serena Williams has won 23 Grand Slam singles titles, held the world No. 1 ranking, and captured multiple Wimbledon singles crowns, while Mariaan de Swardt’s record includes a single WTA singles title and a semifinal appearance in a Grand Slam doubles event.
