{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Liberties (Irish: Na Saoirsí or occasionally Na Libirtí) is an area in central Dublin, Ireland, located to the southwest of the inner city.","title":"The Liberties, Dublin"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" One of Dublin's most historic working-class neighbourhoods, the area is traditionally associated with social housing, market traders, local family-owned businesses, as well as whiskey distilling, it is now also a centre of enterprise and commercial life in the heart of the city.","title":"The Liberties, Dublin"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Today the historic city quarter sits side by side with dynamic media and tech hubs and highly respected medical and education centres.","title":"The Liberties, Dublin"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Brideville Football Club was an Irish association football club, originally based in The Liberties, Dublin.","title":"Brideville F.C."},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They were active during the 1920s, 1930s and 1940s, playing in both the League of Ireland and the Leinster Senior League.","title":"Brideville F.C."},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They were the first ever winners of the FAI Junior Cup and also played in two FAI Cup finals.","title":"Brideville F.C."},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The North Midlands Shield is an annual rugby union knock-out cup club competition organized by the North Midlands Rugby Football Union and was first contested during the 2001-02 season with Ledbury being the first ever winners, defeating Old Saltleians in the final held at Finstall Park in Bromsgrove.","title":"North Midlands Shield"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is currently open for clubs ranked in tier 7-8 of the English rugby union system that fall under the North Midlands RFU umbrella, including sides based in Birmingham and the West Midlands, Herefordshire, Shropshire and Worcestershire.","title":"North Midlands Shield"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" A tier 9 side, Shropshire based Oswestry, who play in Cheshire (South), also take part.","title":"North Midlands Shield"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In 2014 the North Midlands RFU introduced a 'Plate' competition for sides eliminated in the early stages of the Shield.","title":"North Midlands Shield"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" It is one of three men's club competitions in the region along with the North Midlands Cup (for tier 5-6 sides) and the North Midlands Vase (for tier 9-10 sides).","title":"North Midlands Shield"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Stroud and District Combination Bill Adams Cup is an annual rugby union knock-out club competition organized by the Stroud and District Combination - one of the five bodies that make up the Gloucestershire Rugby Football Union.","title":"Stroud and District Combination Bill Adams Cup"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was first introduced during the 1983-84 season, with the first ever winners being Cirencester III.","title":"Stroud and District Combination Bill Adams Cup"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Bill Adams Cup is the third most important cup competition in Stroud District behind the Senior Cup (1st) and Stroud and District Combination Junior Cup (2nd).","title":"Stroud and District Combination Bill Adams Cup"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" With only 9 member clubs the combination is one of the smallest in Gloucestershire.","title":"Stroud and District Combination Bill Adams Cup"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Knock Football Club is a former Irish football club based in east Belfast.","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was founded by members of the Knock Lacrosse Club in 1879 and was the second football club to be formed in Ireland, after Cliftonville F.C..","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It played in the first ever match between two Irish football clubs, when it lost 0-2 to Cliftonville on 1 November 1879.","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was a founding member of the Irish Football Association.","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The club played in the early years of the Irish Cup.","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Its ground was Bloomfield, which in 1882 hosted Ireland's first ever international football match.","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" In 1882, the club changed its name to Down Athletics.","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" By 1891 the club had ceased playing football.","title":"Knock F.C."},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Aldershot Football Club was an English Football League club, which was wound up in the High Court in March 1992.","title":"Aldershot F.C."},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" They became the first Football League club since Accrington Stanley to resign from the League during the course of a season.","title":"Aldershot F.C."},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The club was nicknamed "The Shots" for both the last syllable of the town name and the military links to Aldershot.","title":"Aldershot F.C."},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Aldershot were also the first ever winners of a Football League play-off competition, when they beat Wolverhampton Wanderers in the Division Four play-offs in 1987.","title":"Aldershot F.C."}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: the FAI Junior Cup
